def blog_body(title,subject,evidence,exception,heading,marker):
 return (f'{title} begins by defining one {subject} population, one decision window, the preparing role, and the buyer or provider owner who retains authority. At the {heading.lower()} checkpoint, preserve {evidence} as distinct records instead of compressing them into a status label. Trace {marker}-A identifies source versions, relevant system, time zone, inclusion rule, and safe stopping condition. The support role may collect, compare, schedule, and describe exceptions, but it does not infer commercial, employment, privacy, security, or legal conclusions. A changed request opens a new version so later reviewers can distinguish correction from silent scope expansion. '
         f'The ordinary fixture for {subject} includes complete sources, consistent identifiers, an authorized decision, and a destination receipt. The adverse fixture deliberately introduces the condition where {exception}. Trace {marker}-B keeps both versions, the disputed field, the person asked to decide, the due point, and the dependent action placed on hold. Reconcile population membership before totals or averages; list included, excluded, duplicate, missing, rejected, unresolved, and accepted records. This prevents a polished total from concealing substitution, unsupported exclusion, or work transferred back to the client. '
         f'Access for {title} follows minimum-necessary scope, named accounts, multifactor authentication, approved storage, and a recorded removal path. Synthetic fixtures replace candidate, client, credential, financial, health, and employee data during rehearsal. Trace {marker}-C requires the second reviewer to reproduce the decision from retained evidence without relying on private chat or personal memory. Closure means the approved source agrees with the observed destination state, exceptions have dispositions, and an accountable owner records acceptance. Schedule a recheck after any material provider, system, role, or definition change.')

